---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { collection } from '../../lib/content';
const notes = collection('notes').sort((a, b) => (a.date < b.date ? 1 : -1));
---
<BaseLayout title="Notes — Freightgcc Editorial Analysis" description="Original editorial analysis of China–GCC freight, customs and port economics, with judgement clearly separated from sourced fact.">
  <h1>Notes</h1>
  <p class="article-meta">Original analysis. These are arguments, not news: each note takes a position and shows the reasoning. Regulatory facts are sourced; the judgement is ours.</p>

  <h2>Start with the decision</h2>
  <p>
    These notes are grouped by the decision they change, not by the news cycle. If you are choosing
    a delivery term, start with <a href="/notes/ddp-hides-the-risk/">why DDP quotes hide the risk</a>.
    If you are choosing a gateway, read
    <a href="/notes/port-choice-is-a-customs-choice/">why port choice is a customs choice</a>. If a
    certificate is stuck, the argument is in
    <a href="/notes/saudi-certification-data-problem/">Saudi certification as a data problem</a>.
    For the Red Sea question, start with
    <a href="/notes/red-sea-return/">what the return changes — and what it does not</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What a note is not</h2>
  <p>
    A note is not a news item and not a rate bulletin. It is a dated argument with an exception:
    the case in which the author would not hold the position. The facts should be traceable; the
    judgement is allowed to be disagreed with. If a note is corrected, the change belongs in the
    <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a>, not in a quiet rewrite.
  </p>

  <ul class="note-list">
    {notes.map((n) => (
      <li>
        <p class="article-meta">{n.date} · <a href="/author/wei-wong/">Wei Wong</a></p>
        <h2><a href={`/notes/${n.slug}/`}>{n.title}</a></h2>
        <p>{n.description}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>Our standards are in <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>; fixes are logged in <a href="/corrections/">corrections</a>.</p>
  <h2>How to read a note</h2>
  <p>
    A note is an argument with its evidence attached. The regulatory facts are linked; the judgement
    is labelled as judgement and carries an exception — the case in which the author would not hold
    the position. Notes are dated because the argument can age even when the facts do not.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
